<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mago (agricultural writer)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mago's treatise</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mago was a Carthaginian writer on agriculture who composed a large treatise on farming. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> state that the work, in twenty-eight volumes, was so highly regarded that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> ordered its translation into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>'s destruction in 146 BCE. Through this translation it influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> farming practice for centuries.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Mago wrote his manual in Punic, and it recorded the farming knowledge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, which was then the granary of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. It drew on local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> and Punic practice and covered both agriculture and the care of animals. The Roman writer Columella summarised its opening advice: a man who buys land should sell his town house, so that he will not prefer the household gods of the city to those of the country, since one who delights more in his city residence has no need of a country estate.</p><p>The surviving fragments show the range of the work. They deal with which way the most productive vineyards face, how to plant and prune vines, how to plant olives and fruit trees, how to harvest marsh plants and how to prepare grains and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulses">pulses</a> for grinding. Others concern choosing bullocks, the health of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, farmyard animals, bees and beekeeping, preserving pomegranates and making passum, a raisin wine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Nothing of Mago's own Punic text survives. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, the Carthaginian libraries were given to the kings of Numidia, but Mago's book alone was taken to Rome. Cassius Dionysius adapted it into Greek, and D. Junius Silanus translated it in full into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. The Greek version was later shortened by Diophanes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> into six books.</p><p>What is known of the work comes from quotations of these translations by Roman writers on agriculture, among them Varro, Columella, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> and Gargilius Martialis. Their extracts preserve even small observations, such as the remark that mules sometimes foal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and that mules and mares foal in the twelfth month after conception, as well as the advice that a beekeeper should not kill drones.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mago (agricultural writer)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Carthaginian agricultural science, preserved through the Roman translation of <b>Mago's treatise</b>, influenced Mediterranean farming for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.85,10.31" aria-label="Carthage on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4809 836 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mago (agricultural writer)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5259 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5259 1106h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.85,10.31">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
